Z||000011||reddog||12-14-2001||11:54 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR>[B]He said he wants a "lot" more power. Actually the post is called "need a TON more power".<P><BR> Only answer is what Artherd said, <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A> <P>Yeah that would be good, but good luck finding an engine and paying for the installation for around $5,500.00!!!!!<BR>If this were possible I would have a V8 in both of my GTs.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P> <BR><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dave Gunsul||12-15-2001||12:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by reddog:<BR><B><P><BR> <P>Yeah that would be good, but good luck finding an engine and paying for the installation for around $5,500.00!!!!!<BR>If this were possible I would have a V8 in both of my GTs.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Actaully you can do a V8 for a lot less than that if you do it yourself. The master kit costs $3100 or something like that and Archie's cutting 5% off til x-mas.<BR>Then all you need is an engine and you can<BR>build or order a V8 with the money left over.<P> <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000013||Hairy_Fiero||12-15-2001||12:28 AM||||Well the Caddy swap would be classified in my eyes as a "ton more power" if the redline were substantially increased. I feel a lot of power is lost if the car shifts out at 4800 in every gear. Wouldn't happen to be anyone around the Michigan area who is car inclined? I would be more than happy to pay someone for their services, just not $3,000 worth. Maybe 1000-1500. I could probably tackle the actual engine swap, it's the wiring that would kill me. That would be a killer in my mind to switch virtually every wire in the car.||||reg||11
Z||000014||artherd||12-15-2001||07:59 AM||||Ah.....  If you insist on paying someone else to do the swap turn-key... then you're going to have to nearly double that figure, sorry.<P>Our time isn't free!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: I'd hardly call an increase of 30hp 'a ton more power...' 3.4s are great stock rebuild-up motors, but I doubt anyone on this board would consider an (unmodified) 3.4 a real performance engine...<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000015||Hairy_Fiero||12-15-2001||01:49 PM||||I never said a 30 horse power increase would be a ton more power. In fact I never even brought up the 3.4, and I wouldn't consider even having one. Now I could do the 3.4 swap easily thats plug and play from what I can see, however without a performance rebuild and a turbo charger that would not satisfy me. And to do that it would take a lot more than $5,500 unless I used used crap wich I wouldn't even consider. I've looked over Archie's site and have decided to purchase a kit and attempt a 350 swap myself. Hey if your poor like me you better learn how to tune your own toys if you want to compete because the money sure isn't growing on trees for me. ||||reg||3
Z||000016||West Coast Fiero||12-15-2001||01:51 PM||||well, you get what you pay for<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000017||Archie||12-15-2001||01:53 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hairy_Fiero:<BR><B>I could probably tackle the actual engine swap, it's the wiring that would kill me. That would be a killer in my mind to switch virtually every wire in the car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, IMHO, anything less than a SBC wouldn't be "a TON more power".<P>If you are willing/capable of doing the mechanical work and if you can follow step by step video instructions, then there is no reason you can't do a very good SBC V-8 swap for a lot less than $5500. As far as wiring is concerned our Video also tells you what to do to the stock Fiero harness to make it work with a SBC swap. <P>Our Master-Build V-8 kits runs $3150 (-5% discount mentioned earlier) & includes everything you'll need to mount up a complete SBC engine. You can buy a SBC engine anywhere from $500 for a good used engine or less than $1800 for a NEW LM1 long block. <P>The biggest cost variable in doing a SBC swap is the cost of the engine you choose to use. I would recommend that you read this web page to get an idea of the variety of engine choices you have when swapping in a SBC engine. <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch9.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch9.htm</A> <P>There are several reasons why the choice of a SBC V-8 swap is smarter than some of the other choices. A partial list follows:<P>1) A 3.4 swap only adds about 40 to 50 more HP. At a cost of more than $4K, a 3.4 swap is  not, IMHO, a good return on your investment.<P>2) IMHO, a 3.8 or NStar swap done by someone else is 2 to 3 times more expensive than a SBC swap, you can do yourself. You mention that you worry about the challenge of trying to wire those engines up to work right & that is a valid concern because not a lot of aftermarket wire harnesses are available for those engines. With the SBC swap, everybody and his brother makes an aftermarket wire harness to easily hook up even the most complicated fuel injected SBC engines to any car.<P>3) You say you want a "Ton more power". The most power that any of the other engine choices makes is 300 HP (the 3.4, 3.8, 4.5, & 4.9 all make well less than 300HP. An engine that makes 300 HP or more is real easy to find when you shop for a SBC engine & you can always build your engine up to more than 400 HP (without selling a kidney) later as you have the funds. <P>4) I think that more SBC engines have been swapped into Fieros than all the other choices combined. So the aftermarket support and inexpensive parts that are available make the SBC the best choice again. <P>5) I have done more than 200 of the SBC/Fiero swaps myself and I don't think you can come up with an engine combination choice that I don't have some experience with. Try to find someone doing the V-6 or Caddy swaps that can honestly tell you that he has experience with each and every combination of the particular engine type that he is advocating.<P>Now 20 people are going to try to Flame me, but I didn't really write this to put them down. This is written to let you know that a SBC swap can be done for the same kind of money that some of the little V-6's can be done for.<P>I hope this helps.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroX||12-15-2001||01:58 PM||||3800 supercharged V6.  If you can do the work yourself, you will be in around $3500.  Watch my website as I detail this swap to the T.  No install kits, no five figure amounts.<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroX||12-15-2001||02:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>  anything less than a SBC wouldn't be "a TON more power".........little V-6's.....<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>bring it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Dave Gunsul||12-15-2001||04:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B> bring it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Although I do have a lot of respect for the 3800 swap I wouldn't make that challenge if I were you because the SBC can be built in an<BR>almost infinate varriety of ways. If you have<BR>a place to do the swap then the SBC is the way to go and i'll tell you why. With most other swaps you ask: how much HP can I get out of this engine? With a SBC you have to ask: how much power do I really need.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>There's so many performance parts for the SBC<BR>that it is almost staggering and when you put<BR>an engine like that into such a light little car then you get an immediate road terror that is literally capable of destroying almost everything in it's path.<BR>Now before anyone flames me remember that I have built a V6 and have not done the V8 swap<BR>because I don't have anywhere to do it. I am very happy with how my V6 has come out and i'm still not done with it AND it is very quick but I certainly won't say that it is better than a V8 Fiero. It was just the best way for me to go and I think that that is what everyone needs to decide: what is best for them.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
