Z||000035||Bikemanj||12-15-2001||04:43 PM||||Hey dave once is enough. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I have to agree with fierox. The supercharged 3800 has lots of potential to keep up with most SBC's. The really built up SBC's will stomp anything but now you have $4000 in the motor. With a $100 pulley swap (to a 3.4") you can ad about 45Hp to the 3800 and it starts with 240Hp and 270# factory. It has the advantage of producing less heat than a SBC and is very reliable. If you want use the automaitc that comes with the motor the swap is quite easy and its a good automatic...if you like autos.<BR>Just another opinion.<BR>Then again you CAN'T go wrong with a LT1 from Archie. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drool.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000036||SLYGUY||12-15-2001||05:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bikemanj:<P><B>Then again you CAN'T go wrong with a LT1 from Archie.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drool.gif"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>From what I've read so far Archie is your man. Tons of power, torque, reliability, and experience doing these swaps. Contact him. <P>Sly<P>P.S. Will you have to smog the car up there in MN? Keep that in mind.||||reg||1
Z||000037||87fastback||12-16-2001||01:14 PM||||Why is everyone so bent on putting a big HP engine in the back of the Fiero?  Too many times I see guys put the biggest cams, NOS and all this other stuff into an engine build and forget one VERY important issue - once you have all this new found power How are you going to get it to the ground?<P>I am all for having a nice sounding SBC with tons of power, but it really does no good if you are left spinning at the lights.  I trully believe that if you put $5000 into engine mods, you should be doing the same to the suspension!  500 HP means nothing when you have stock springs, struts, axles and everything else.  Try not to be blinded by HP and look at the BIG picture.  Do a coil over conversion, better tires, sway bars and maybe look at weight distribution when all is said and done.<P>I love small blocks and would love to have a 500 HP monster in my Fiero, but from a handling standpoint won't ever do it.  I would rather go towards the 3800SC or Northstar.  Have you ever driven a car with those engines?  They are really nothing short of amazing, and these are stock!!<P>The 4.9 is really for the person limited on funds and still want the V8.  Take a good look at the Northstar or 3800SC, the 3800SC is a reall screamer in stock form and it won't hinder on the weight and handling of the car.<P>Maybe you could give us some more specifics on the car like what its intended uses are and such.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000038||northstarfieroorbust||12-16-2001||01:20 PM||||go with the caddy, you can get some major reliability as well as a powerful engine and 5500 should cover it all||||reg||1
Z||000039||Hairy_Fiero||12-16-2001||02:06 PM||||Ok heres some info on my 87Gt 5spd. Suspension wise in the front has Eibach lowering springs 1" and Koni shocks. New 1" front sway bar. Rear also has heavy duty sway bar, Koni rear struts and the Eibach lowering springs 1". All bushings replaced with poly in front and back also. Replaced rack and pinion with new and added a steering stabilizer. Car has 16" rims with z-rated Pirelli's. All of this was replaced 260 miles ago.<P>Brakes setup is crossed drilled rotors and rebuilt calipers all around. Brakes have 0 miles still need to adjust them. <P>This is why I only have 5500 left for the powerplant! Was seriously considering the archie kit and a 350 swap, however the 4.9 swap is also very appealing. Would like some pro's and con's between having the two. Obviously The caddy will be lighter, and not as powerful.. however cheaper it appears. Is the Caddy good on gas? I can imagine a 350 without TPI would be horrible.<P>Anyway I hope my suspension and brake upgrades are optimal when considering a v8 conversion. Anybodies thoughts and suggestions are greatly appreciated.||||reg||3
Z||000040||fieroX||12-16-2001||02:24 PM||||My car will be nothing short of astounding.  Im not going to speculate on what it will run, but I do have an idea.  I will be able to get 35+ mpg on the highway, and as for vettes, vipers and the such, later!||||reg||1
Z||000041||Slammed Fiero||12-16-2001||03:04 PM||||This forum always amazes me , Everyone is building a car goes Sooooo Damm fast!!!! Regaurdless of what motor their putting in there going to beat vettes and porsches and Vipers.<P>Then thats the last you hear of them.. they have all these marvelous ideas , with comments such as...Ohhh You should see mine , I Guesstimate 700hp , because desktop dyno told me so , and my uncle has this frined who had the same motor and it ran 7's in the 1/4<P>I need to buy a good shovel and some Hip waiters for some of the BS I see. It's all fine and dandy on paper , but lets see it on the street.<P>Kudos to those of you who actually put up. You go to they dynos and the strip and get real performance data / Hp figures.<P>Phil<BR>Tina<BR>Chris moore<BR>Jay<BR>Banditbalz<BR>3800NOS<BR>Lildevil<BR>There are a bunch of you 3800SC boys serious about your power!<P>These are my thoughts...take them however you like.<P>Personally I would have a hard time choosing between a 3.8SC and a small block. Both have their stong points.<P>Jonathan McCreery<BR>Mid 15's in the 1/4 , probable low 14's on  the bottle.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||KissMySSFiero||12-16-2001||03:25 PM||||for anyone who stated that a sc3800 or northstar wont effect your handling because of the extra weight is wrong.  <BR>people want to think because a sc3800 is only six cylinders it doesnt weigh more, but its a larger motor(90* not 60*) and you have a blower on top of it.  <BR>people also thing the northstar is lighter because its all aluminum.  well that big a$$ 4T80E tranny isnt exactly petite.  now a nStar mated to a getrag would be light.  but who has one that runs and how much did it cost you.  <P>I say hit the lotto,   but a new aluminum small block chevy and build away.  Its only about 8grand for the block.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>Two 88FORMULAS Red & Yellow<BR>        <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>         <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000043||fieroX||12-16-2001||04:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>This forum always amazes me , Everyone is building a car goes Sooooo Damm fast!!!! Regaurdless of what motor their putting in there going to beat vettes and porsches and Vipers.<P>Then thats the last you hear of them.. they have all these marvelous ideas , with comments such as...Ohhh You should see mine , I Guesstimate 700hp , because desktop dyno told me so , and my uncle has this frined who had the same motor and it ran 7's in the 1/4<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I appreciate your criticism, it only pushes me that much more.  As for hearing the last of me, no way.  Ill be here for a long time.  I will have my car on the hot rod power tour.  Ill be at the Grand Prix shootout, which may have High Performance Pontiac there.  Im going to the fiero nationals, the power fest if it happens, and the oklahoma power fest.  Im going to have my website forever, Im going to have videos of my races on there.  <BR>But I guess I can say whatever I want on the internet.  So Ill leave it at that, next summer there is going to be a fiero roaming around this country, making as many stops as possible.  Everyone will see.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroX.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000044||Slammed Fiero||12-16-2001||04:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>for anyone who stated that a sc3800 or northstar wont effect your handling because of the extra weight is wrong.  <BR>people want to think because a sc3800 is only six cylinders it doesnt weigh more, but its a larger motor(90* not 60*) and you have a blower on top of it.  <BR>people also thing the northstar is lighter because its all aluminum.  well that big a$$ 4T80E tranny isnt exactly petite.  now a nStar mated to a getrag would be light.  but who has one that runs and how much did it cost you.  <P>I say hit the lotto,   but a new aluminum small block chevy and build away.  Its only about 8grand for the block.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Keep in mind that even thogh your adding weight to the Fiero , it's still lighter than a vette , viper or new 911. Each of those is about 400-500lbs heavier than a Aluminum head 350.<P>Power to weight is where it's at.<P>JM<P>||||reg||1
Z||000045||artherd||12-16-2001||08:41 PM||||Go for it man!!  Get Archie's master build kit, then frankly if you can change an engine, you can install a V8!<P>If you're crafty, it'll all come in under $5500, but it's going to take a while.<P>It'll be the expierence of a lifetime though!!<P>If wiring is your only worry, don't !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Archie has it all figured out, and his video will guide you through it.  If all else fails, call him, he'll tell you "purple wire THERE... start!")<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hairy_Fiero:<BR><B>I never said a 30 horse power increase would be a ton more power. In fact I never even brought up the 3.4, and I wouldn't consider even having one. Now I could do the 3.4 swap easily thats plug and play from what I can see, however without a performance rebuild and a turbo charger that would not satisfy me. And to do that it would take a lot more than $5,500 unless I used used crap wich I wouldn't even consider. I've looked over Archie's site and have decided to purchase a kit and attempt a 350 swap myself. Hey if your poor like me you better learn how to tune your own toys if you want to compete because the money sure isn't growing on trees for me. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000046||artherd||12-16-2001||08:54 PM||||Thanks, I'm getting tired of all the bench racing myself :P<P>(PS: are you subtly trying to tell me "build your dam car Ben, come on, do it already!" ? ? ;P)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>This forum always amazes me , Everyone is building a car goes Sooooo Damm fast!!!! Ohhh You should see mine , I Guesstimate 700hp , because desktop dyno told me so , and my uncle has this frined who had the same motor and it ran 7's in the 1/4</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000047||Joe Torma||12-16-2001||09:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Thanks, I'm getting tired of all the bench racing myself :P<P>(PS: are you subtly trying to tell me "build your dam car Ben, come on, do it already!" ? ? ;P)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not out loud, at least.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000048||Dave Gunsul||12-16-2001||11:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bikemanj:<BR><B>Hey dave once is enough.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>WOW! I don't know what happened there,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> I didn't do anything different than usual but both my above posts got screwed up somehow.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Sorry everybody about all those extra posts!<P><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||11
Z||000049||fieroX||12-17-2001||03:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Thanks, I'm getting tired of all the bench racing myself :P<P>(PS: are you subtly trying to tell me "build your dam car Ben, come on, do it already!" ? ? ;P)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think that was directed at my comment about beating vettes and vipers.  Thats fine.  I love it.||||reg||1
Z||000050||Formula88||12-17-2001||12:35 PM||||I think Ben has said it best in the past.  Go for which engine you LIKE.  A properly done N*, 3800SC, or SBC will all be fast as nuts.  The N* will likely be the most expensive, and the SBC has the potential to be the cheapest (especially if you get a used engine).<P>Like Archie has pointed out, people say it'll cost $4000 for a SBC engine, but they can get a 3800SC for only $1500 (not this thread, but I've heard the argument before).  Well, first off, I've not found one that cheap, and they're used.  That $4000 price tag is for a NEW SBC with warranty from GM.  Compare apples to apples, people.  You can get used SBC's everywhere for under $1000.<P>Just from memory, I've read about Jay's N* running 13.6 in the quarter, Tina's 383 SBC running in the 11.9's, and a few mid 12's by some 3800SC's.  Take that at face value.  Your mileage may vary.  That 13.6 for the N* is probably not indicative of what one CAN do, but I've not seen a faster time posted by a N* yet. ||||reg||1
Z||000051||BOILERMAKER||12-17-2001||01:33 PM||||Add me and BENGAL to the list of 3800SC boys.  Mine is only 100 miles into the fresh build, but the dyno isn't far away.||||reg||1
Z||000052||Slammed Fiero||12-17-2001||01:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B> I think that was directed at my comment about beating vettes and vipers.  Thats fine.  I love it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just a general comment...By all means , if you plan to document the construction of your monster you backing up your claims<P>In anycase...<P>Choosing a motor is just about the most intimate decision you can make when you build a car. I think it says a lot about the owner. It took me 6 months to choose my wheels alone. We can all argue Torque and HP until the cows come home. It won't help you make your decision. If I had money in hand I would be looking around my area for people with the conversions I was looking at. Go talk to them , ride in their cars, See what you think. Each motor has it's own merits , research it yourself , don't listen to us..we will likley confuse the hell out of you (haha).<P>Just remember , do what you want , do it once and Do it right. No hack jobs.<P>Jonathan McCreery<BR>Just as confused as the rest of us about what motor is best.<BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000053||fieroX||12-17-2001||01:56 PM||||It took me about 4 months to decide on which engine I wanted.  I started off wanting the 3800SC but then I started looking into doing a 3.4 turbo.  After I started adding it all up I decided to do a series I 3800 and turbo it, and after I added all of that up I went back to the 3800 SC.  A good friend of mine has one of these engines in his 91 Lumina.  Ive riden in the car thousands of miles, and driven it a few times, and once down the strip!  He told me he could help me do the swap, and after doing the swap into his own car he knows everything about these engines.  Now so do I.  Thats how I decided, but you guys are right, you have to research each swap, and do the one that suits you the best.  I just have a feeling after I get this swap finished and get some videos on my site, there is going to be alot more people wanting 3800SC's.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000054||Zoom88||12-17-2001||03:34 PM||||Personally I will be happy with a 3.4 stroker with a good cam, and spend all my money on the suspension and weight distribution. I love running and powering out of a fast curve and honing my driving skills. I could really care less what it will do in a quarter mile (I use to have a 69 Chevelle for that, got kinda boring). It is hard to beat a good handling car attached to a good driver no matter how many horses you have.......and yes under the above mentioned conditions/scenerio I have smoked many a V8 (not that it really matters, its all in the rush for me !!) It all comes down to how hard are you willing to push it babeeeeee....... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000055||Bikemanj||12-18-2001||03:46 AM||||Whatever you do, I must recommend using computer controlled fuel injection. Drivablility, emissions and HP are all superrior to anything with a carb. Pick an egngine with enough factory power and dont try to reinvent the internal combustion engine with huge valves, cams, compressinon, headers, etc.<P>The S/C 3800, SBC, 4.9 Caddy, 3.4 DOHC, and even the little quad 4 are good choices. Northstars really need the automatic and cost alot. I love the S/C 3800 but each engine has its advantages and disadvantages.<BR>Study them all and have fun with the one you pick.||||reg||12
Z||000056||elfiero||12-18-2001||08:07 AM||||My .02,<BR> I HAD a tpi-sbc 87GT.First i had a 4L60 <BR>automatic-did'nt like it.I switched to a 5spd<BR>isuzu,shortend shifter,faster clutch linkage,<BR>all the "cool stuff",still did'nt like it.<BR>Sold it and all my fiero stuff-BOUGHT A VETTE<BR>I like it.||||reg||12
Z||000057||Hairy_Fiero||12-18-2001||08:25 AM||||Yea thats great, I suggest you go post on a Vette forum then.||||reg||1
Z||000058||jd2m4||12-18-2001||10:20 AM||||That's funny!!||||reg||7
Z||000059||88VortechGT||12-18-2001||10:26 AM||||Well not to add to the HP guessing, but I built a 3.3 liter V-6 that is going in my 88 GT and the HP numbers at the crank are well over 350HP, and no I did not use desktop dyno to figure this out. My engine builder (who worked for John Lingenfelter) has a fully operational testing bay and he ran my engine for at least a hour to get the dyno plots for the engine. And just to clarufy this engine has cost me about $12,000 to build, and it also is using a Vortech V-9 Series Supercharger, I am not bragging I am just stating that you can get 300 plus from a small pushrod V-6, it just takes lots of time and engineering to do it. I would post the dyno plots but since I am on a Macintosh system I can not post pics. But if anyone has any questions feel free to ask, I would be more than happy to convey the information onto others and help them achieve similiar results.<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>88 GT Vortech Supercharged 3.3 W/350HP<BR>02 Subaru WRX AWD-T 230HP||||reg||6
Z||000060||1FST2M6||12-18-2001||10:39 AM||||sorry if i sound like a prick but come on!  i've had to fix some of lingenfelters 383 Camaros all with trashed blocks and warped heads. (3 of them) <P>WHY does anyone make claims of HP? I don't get it.. i'd put $ ..no i'll pay for your dyno runs if you even break 300hp. you plan on running an intercooled 15-20psi?<P><BR>I've got a 3.8 with a  440t4 tranny if anyones interested... ran 1 weekago when i pulled it out of the car. $500.(plus shipping)  not insane SBC torque but MUCH better than a 2.8-3.4.  <P>Nitrous is a great idea. it's cheap and doens't effect your daily drivability. you get your normal fuel consumptions mon-friday.. then saturday night your flirting with 12s in the 1/4 and 0-60 times in the sub 5 second range.. all for under a grand. you can fill one bottle a week for over 2 years before a turbo would pay off and with $5500 to drop... you can fill one bottle a week for 42 months! PLUS when your tired of the car you can pull the system out and carry it over to the next toy. be it a mini van a Turbo diesel New Beetle or whatever blows your dress up at the time. <P><BR>just my nickles worth.   <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst2m6sig_1.gif"> <P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...||||reg||1
Z||000061||msweldon||12-18-2001||11:24 AM||||I'm not a "big" fan of nitrous, but riding in 1FST's GT definately made me a believer out of the potential of a properly setup NOS kit.  It's like "insta-power", and if your on a budget, you can't beat it. One of my co-workers has a C5 vette and 1fst's gt has more neck snapping acceleration off the line.<P>Whew!! Gotta go with trav on this one too, 350hp out of a superchaged 3.3, you better be intercooling that baby or say good bye to your ring-lands, and where are you gonna mount a intercooler in a fiero to reduce that much of a air-charge.||||reg||1
Z||000062||80's BOY||12-18-2001||12:39 PM||||well, to Slammed Fiero i enjoyed your post about how everyone is gonna do this and gonna do that, blah blah blah. then you contradict yourself by stating " probable 14's in the 1/4 mile" under your signature. i mean, if you don't know then don't state it until it's a fact. as for more power, the v8 is definitely in my opinion the choice for alot of us. some do it; others dream about it; alot want it but simply don't have the time or shop space to do so. i say to all of you out there who claim for fame, put up or shut up. and by the way my Fiero's ass is in the air right now while i prepare the engine bay for the v8 transplant. Archie, you will be hearing from me soon. to all others, good luck with whatever project you decide upon. it's YOUR car for god's sake. do with it what you like.<P><BR>Brian Olsovsky<BR>1985 GT Targa<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000063||Slammed Fiero||12-18-2001||01:03 PM||||I just base my low 14's on the fact that Guys running the identical NOS setup I have on cars with auto's were netting low 14's , so my assumption is probably pessimistic since I have a 4spd with 3.65 gears instead of the Auto's 3.3(?) ratio, plus I run Pirelli DOT slicks.<P>My assumptions about my performance figures are well back by others running NOS, they are also not outlandish like some people you see on the Forum. People have to keep in mind that were not the only ones modifying our cars. <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000064||88VortechGT||12-18-2001||01:04 PM||||It's funny how you guys are so closed minded and think the 350 HP is impossible with  a 3.3. That's fine believe what you want, but I have the numbers to prove it. Plus you should read a little more into the post, I have 350 at the crank which means it would be around 280-290 at the wheels taking in account about a 15-20% drivetrain loss. I guess you guys also find it hard to believe that there are 1,000 HP 4 bangers running 7's and 8's at the strip too. And yes it will be intercooled and running about 12 psi. Oh, and as far as my engine builders ability, let's just say that he has forgotten more about engine building than most of you will ever know.||||reg||1
Z||000065||80's BOY||12-18-2001||02:53 PM||||Vortech, i have to give you some respect. for a guy with 8 posts you come on pretty strong. you probably have a really bad ass motor there and you seem to know it. but why haven't you posted here in the past?? i mean alot of these bench racers would pick you to death to know about a supercharged Fiero engine. also anybody that's been to the NIRA Drag Wars can see the potential for 4cyl engines if you have the money and the means. i would really like to know more about your engine and possible completion date. i hope to have my car together by next racing season. it's been really wet here in Texas lately so i can't do too much to the car right now. damn muddy road. i'd also like to know how much boost you plan to run and how you will control it. i tossed around the supercharger idea for a few months last year but decided it would take too much engineering.||||reg||14
Z||000066||88VortechGT||12-18-2001||03:23 PM||||I have been on the Fiero List for about 5 Years and I have been building Fiero's since I was 16. I also have pictures of the motor if you would like me to email you. I have a complete work up of the engine, but becuase of it's size I will just to do a brief overview of the engine internals: 2.8 Liter Block stroked to a 3.1 and over bored to a 3.3, a Custom Ground Forged Crank, Forged H-Beam Connecting rods, Forged and ceramic coated pistons with floating wristpins. Heads exhaust and intake are portmatched as well as ported and polished with Titanium sodium filled valves, V-8 Valve springs with custom ground seats as well as a multi-angle valve job, high performance retainers, roller rockers and lifters with a custom ground camshaft. as well as everything was balanced and blue printed, including the flywheel and clutch assembly. I am running 10:1 compression with Electromotive's Tec 2 and as you can guess this thing will not run on pump gas. I don't think my motor is the greatest thing on earth I am just trying to prove that you can get over 300Hp out of a smaller push rod V-6, but one of the drawbacks is it just takes a lot of time and money. I hope to have the car back on the road this summer so I can get some 1/4 mile times as well as some chassis dyno numbers.<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>88 GT Vortech Supercharged 3.3 W/350HP<BR>02 Subaru WRX AWD-T 230HP||||reg||10
Z||000067||Joe Torma||12-18-2001||03:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88VortechGT:<BR><B>...with Electromotive's Tec 2...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey another TECII guy, cool.  Your motor sounds like a lil monster to me...send me pics!||||reg||1
Z||000068||80's BOY||12-18-2001||05:32 PM||||Damn! so how much boost??||||reg||14
Z||000069||joplinfiero||12-18-2001||09:08 PM||||Hey 88VortechGT,<P>Can you tell use what the spec's are for the cam that you put in that engine??<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000070||fieroX||12-18-2001||09:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B><BR>......they are also not outlandish like some people you see on the Forum. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000071||Standard||12-18-2001||09:43 PM||||One correction.. Tina's car is a 383 chevy sb.  She's not a boy, either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Hairy, we have a pretty good mix of swapped Fiero's up here.. 3.1, 3.4, 3800, 3800SC, a few v8's, my quad 4... little late in the season for a get-together (unless the weather stays like it is) but if you haven't decided by next spring I'm sure there'll be a meet where you can ride in a few different engined Fiero's.  Personally, I say go with a v8.  Just seeing the crowds that gather around Tina's car when we stop at Porky's or red rock was enough to convince me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_sig_pic_1.jpg">        <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000072||88VortechGT||12-19-2001||08:12 AM||||I am running a range of 10.5 - 13.0 PSI after I work out all the bugs with the Tec-II, like fuel mapping, ignition retarding, Fuel pressure adjustments. So I can get the optimum performance. I also have to finish the roll cage and some exterior work as well as a Wilwood brake upgrades.<P>I don't have my cam spec card with me but I can find out. It's not a real agressive cam but it is set up for forced induction that way I can get the boost and fuel in as quick as possible but still have a decent enough flow on the exhaust side to allow it to breathe as well.||||reg||1
Z||000073||1FST2M6||12-19-2001||12:36 PM||||not closed minded just realistic. i watch people on about 10 forums and at the shop  more than 90% are talk. the rare occasion somone ponys up i'm willing to back down.  I'm a beliver once i see dyno results either dynojet or water break numbers or a time slip till then don't quote #s. <P>I work at a Dyno tuning and custom fabrication facility and have built and been a part of LOTS of different set ups... very few V6s unless it's a buick will ever see life above 300hp... <BR>spraying, turbo or s/c (roots or centrifugal) even the 20psi 3l DOHC sho, only makes 340FWhp. the GM DOHC is beautiful under a turbo. cause it has a decent head... our 60*  aluminum and steel are horrible. <P><BR>and Thanks Msweldon... (you've got mail)   <P><BR>Vortech- Sorry to jump on you but you cought me at a bad time and i'm tired of all the talk. RSM claims just over 200 and your set up although you'll have real engine management is very similar. Yep i'm skeptical. but best of luck and please keep me posted... if you can break 300 at the wheels i'll pay for your dyno run. ||||reg||1
Z||000074||88VortechGT||12-19-2001||02:16 PM||||Yes, your right RSM posts 200HP on a stock 2.8 not a race prepped 3.3 and sure I will be more than glad to Dyno my car but, I never said I had 300HP at the wheels I Said I had 350HP at the crank. and with a 15-20% drivetrain loss that puts me around 280-290 at the wheels. I have not chassis dynoed my car yet but I had the engine tested in a test bay where they run the engine indepently to get the test dyno plots. I am not one of those ricers who claim 300Hp because I have an intake and a Cat Back exhaust on my car. Those types pi** me off to no end because they give the legimate motorhead a bad reputation and trust me I have to put up with them all the time when I am in my WRX even though I don't waste my gas trying to prove something to someone with a soup can exhaust tip and sh*t load of stickers up the side of their car.||||reg||1
Z||000075||artherd||12-20-2001||01:22 AM||||That's a pretty shody drivetrain you've got there if it's dropping 20%!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I'd expect to see not more than 10-15% total driveline losses on a chassis dyno with almost any Fiero.<P>I'd love to see your engine dyno curves, if you email me them, I will post them for you!<P> &gt; ben@cartsys.com &lt;<P>Sounds exciting, that's a heluva lotta power from basically the 2.8 block. You're doing it right, fordged everything, and the TEC-II is gooood, you won't blow it the first week with one of those! (though I gotta admit, I'm concerned about long-term reliability.  Are you building a mainly race/drag/show car, or are you planning to drive her fairly often on the road and rack up miles? <P>PS: That Subaru is one SUV I'd actually own!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88VortechGT:<BR><B>Yes, your right RSM posts 200HP on a stock 2.8 not a race prepped 3.3 and sure I will be more than glad to Dyno my car but, I never said I had 300HP at the wheels I Said I had 350HP at the crank. and with a 15-20% drivetrain loss that puts me around 280-290 at the wheels. I have not chassis dynoed my car yet but I had the engine tested in a test bay where they run the engine indepently to get the test dyno plots. I am not one of those ricers who claim 300Hp because I have an intake and a Cat Back exhaust on my car. Those types pi** me off to no end because they give the legimate motorhead a bad reputation and trust me I have to put up with them all the time when I am in my WRX even though I don't waste my gas trying to prove something to someone with a soup can exhaust tip and sh*t load of stickers up the side of their car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000076||88VortechGT||12-20-2001||09:36 AM||||No this will be a weekend track car, and maybe I'll show it. The drive train figure's were given to me by the builder they are just an average drivtrain loss, they are not a exact figure. I will scan my dyno curves this weekend and email you a .jpeg of them then if you can post them for me that would be great. I am using a Getrag Tranny with a Modified Quaife L.S.D. and custom built Heavy Duty axles and C.V. Joints.<P>Yes, I love my WRX especially since it's stock and can smoke a lot of hooked up imports, I love dropping the clutch at 6,000 RPM all you hear is a little chirp and and the car launches at full boost and pins you back in the seat. LOL||||reg||1
Z||000077||Shaun41178||12-20-2001||11:38 AM||||look below<p>[This message has been edited by Shaun41178 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000078||Shaun41178||12-20-2001||11:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88VortechGT:<BR><B> My engine builder (who worked for John Lingenfelter) has a fully operational testing bay and he ran my engine for at least a hour to get the dyno plots for the engine. And just to clarufy this engine has cost me about $12,000 to build, and it also is using a Vortech V-9 Series Supercharger,<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>you state that you had your car on a engine dyno for over an hour to get 350 hp but you later say in another post that you have to tune it more to fine tune it.  Why didnt' you just have that done on the engine dyno while it was all hooked up?  Maybe you didnt' want to which is a good excuse I guess but it just  would have made sense to do it that way.  Then just drop it in the car with that same program and off you.  Any reason for that?<P>Also where did you get your crank?  What kind of rods did you use?  I saw you said H-beam but what brand?  What brand pistons?  Where did you get your titanium valves that are sodium filled?  I mean thats Z06 technology there.  You state roller lifters, so that means a roller cam.  Where did you get your roller cam?  And numbers on cam would be great like someone mentioned earlier.<P>I don't know why you chose to run such high compression with a boosted motor.  Its your motor of course so thats your decision but if it was me I would have gone no higher then 9-1 which would allow pump gas driveability plus the option to run more boost in the future.  More boost will make more hp over more compression anyday of the week and twice on sunday.<P>I am not a skeptic nor am I trying to be an @ss by asking these questions.  I am asking cause I have something similar in the works.  <P>I have a 3100 I am working on.  I have been told the stock crank can handle well over 300 hp.  Which means I am not going to go with a forged unit like you did.  Pretty much bottom end is staying factory except the pistons.  PRobably go forged with that but thats it as the stock rods are forged as well.<P>Gen 3 heads flow over 200 cfm at .500 lift.  Or so I am told.  I have yet to see flow charts but I am waiting on them.  So air flow shouldnt' be a problem.  Once I get flow numbers I will post them here for all to see.<P>  Its the titanium valves that interest me the most.  Like where did you get them and how much were they?  I am sure you have receipts laying around still for insurance purposes.  I am interested in valves like this too for the lightness of them.  Since I am using a all roller setup I am trying to keep everything as light as possible int he valvetrain and the valves would take a lot of weight down.  Also wondering why that if you went with titanium valves you didn't go with titanium retainers and keepers?  That would have dropped the valvetrain weight down too.  Better then that steel you have now. <P>Oh yea its all being bolted to an aluminum block too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I appreciate any info you can give me about the above questions. Thanks<P><P>------------------<BR>SOLD 85 GT w/ nitrous.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000079||Shaun41178||12-21-2001||12:57 PM||||won't let this die.  TTT||||reg||1
Z||000080||Hairy_Fiero||12-21-2001||03:05 PM||||I also like Shaun, would like to hear where you purchased titanium sodium filled valves?? something I would be very very interested in.||||reg||1
Z||000081||DKOV||12-21-2001||04:46 PM||||Sodium filled Valves are not new Z06 technology...  Lotus has been using them on their Esprit Turbos since around the late 70s.  Even today, they are still used on their Esprit Turbos (Mine has them) and most probably on the new Elise too.<P>I imgine they can be had for any car, provided you want to spend the money.<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
